# ── Line endings, pinned ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (it is load-bearing, not housekeeping):
#
# The scored image runs `RUN ./build.sh` inside Linux. Git decides a file's
# line endings at CHECKOUT time, from the cloner's own configuration -- not
# from the committed bytes. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` as the
# SYSTEM default, so a fresh `git clone` of this repository on a stock Windows
# box rewrites every shell script to CRLF, and then:
#
#     $ docker build ... track-a
#     /bin/sh: 1: ./build.sh: not found            (or: bad interpreter: ^M)
#
# That is a silent zero on the track, and it is invisible on this machine
# because a local `core.autocrlf=false` was set here and `.git/config` is
# never committed. Pinning the endings in a tracked file is the only fix that
# survives a clone.
#
# Default: text normalised to LF in the repository, and written back as LF on
# every checkout, whatever the cloner's git config says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The files whose bytes are executed directly by the scored build, or parsed
# by a tool that would treat a stray CR as data. Forced explicitly rather than
# left to content detection.
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.py         text eol=lf
*.ps1        text eol=lf
*.rs         text eol=lf
*.toml       text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf

# ── Byte-exact exceptions (later rules win) ───────────────────────────────

# The organizers' vendored tree is byte-identical by contract: its hashes are
# checked against upstream. Marked binary so git never rewrites a byte of it,
# on commit or on checkout.
/reference/** -text

# The Track C tiny corpus is deliberately hostile. It mixes CRLF and bare LF
# record terminators (~489 / ~651) because section 2.2 of the spec requires a
# parser to handle both, and it carries a UTF-8 BOM. Those bytes ARE the test;
# normalising them to LF would silently delete the coverage on the track the
# organizers call the DQ minefield. Never rewritten.
/verification/cases/track-c/records.csv -text

# Profiles are opaque binary data.
*.profdata binary
*.profraw  binary
